At temperature, $T_{1}=300 \mathrm{ K}$
Average kinetic energy, $E_{1}=6.21 \times 10^{-21} \mathrm{ J}$
\therefore \quad T_{2}=600 $\mathrm{ K},$ E_{2}=$\text{ ? }$ We know that, average kinetic energy of gas molecule, $\begin{aligned}
\Rightarrow \quad\frac{E_{2}}{E_{1}}$&=$\frac{T_{2}}{T_{1}} \Rightarrow$ E_{2}=$\frac{T_{2}}{T_{1}} \times$ E_{1} \\
&=$\frac{600}{300} \times6.21 \times10^{-21} \\ \text{$ and $} =2 \times6.21 \times10^{-21} \\ \text{ and } =12.42 \times10^{-21} \mathrm{ J}
\end{aligned}$

We know that $P V=\underline{n} R T \begin{gathered}
\therefore$ n=$\frac{m}{\text{ moleculer mass }}=\frac{5}{32}$\\
\Rightarrow \quad P V=$\left(\frac{5}{32}\right)$ R T
\end{gathered}

Required heat, $Q \propto r^{2}$
where $r$ is the radius. $\begin{aligned}
\therefore \quad\frac{Q_{1}}{Q_{2}}$&=$\frac{r_{1}^{2}}{r_{2}^{2}}$\\
$\frac{Q_{1}}{Q_{2}}$&=$\frac{(1.5)^{2}}{(1)^{2}}$\\
$\frac{Q_{1}}{Q_{2}}$&=$\frac{9}{4}
\end{aligned}$

The average velocity

$\begin{aligned}$$v_{\mathrm{av}$} &=$\frac{v_{1}+v_{2}+v_{3} \ldots v_{n}}{N}$\\
&=$\frac{1+3+5+7}{4}=$4 $\mathrm{~km}$/ $\mathrm{s}
\end{aligned}$Root mean square velocity

$v_{\mathrm{rms}$}=$\sqrt{\frac{v_{1}^{2}+v_{2}^{2}+v_{3}^{2}+v_{4}^{2}+\ldots+v_n^{2}}{N}}

\begin{array}{l}
=\sqrt{\frac{1+(3)^{2}+(5)^{2}+(7)^{2}}{4}}$\\
=$\sqrt{21}=$4.583 $\mathrm{~km}$/ $\mathrm{s}
\end{array}$Difference between average velocity and root mean square velocity $=4.583-4$
=0.583 $\mathrm{~km}$/ $\mathrm{s}$

The amount of heat required to increase the internal energy is
$\left(\mathrm{Q}-\frac{\mathrm{Q}}{3}\right)=\frac{2}{3} \mathrm{Q}$
For a diatomic gas, the amount of heat required to increase the internal energy is $C_{v}=\frac{5}{2}R$\begin{array}{l}
\therefore \ffrac{2}{3}Q=$\frac{5}{2}$R \\
\therefore Q=$\frac{15}{4}$R
\end{array}

$\begin{aligned}& U=U_1+U_2 \\
& T=\frac{\left(P_1 V_1+P_2 V_2\right) T_1 T_2}{\left(P_1 V_1 T_2+P_2 V_2 T_1\right)}
\end{aligned}$

For a gas mixture, C_V=$\frac{n_1 C_{V_1}+n_2 C_{V_2}}{n_1+n_2} \text{ and }$ C_p=$\frac{n_1 C_{p_1}+n_2 C_{p_2}}{n_1+n_2}$ For monoatomic gas, n=n_1, C_{V_1}=$\frac{3}{2}$ R, \quad C_{p_1}=$\frac{5}{2}$ R For diatomic gas, n=n_2, C_{V_2}=$\frac{5}{2}$ R $\text{ and }$ C_{p_2}=$\frac{7}{2}$ R Now given for gas mixture, $\begin{gathered}
\quad\frac{C_p}{C_V}=1.5 \\
\text{ So, }1.5=\frac{\frac{n_1}{n_2}\left(\frac{5}{2}\right)+\frac{7}{2}}{\frac{n_1}{n_2}\left(\frac{3}{2}\right)+\frac{5}{2}} \quad \frac{9}{2}  \left(\frac{n_1}{n_2}\right)+\frac{15}{2}=\left(\frac{n_1}{n_2}\right)$5+7 \\
\Rightarrow $\frac{n_1}{n_2}=1
\end{gathered}
